A||N||8||862M4inCA||Cross Country drive....odd things to check?||||5||
Z||000000||862M4inCA||10-01-2000||03:12 PM||||Well guys....going to be taking my baby for a little drive from California to Texas and was wondering if there is anything engine/transaxle related that I should check to ensure a trouble free drive. The car has a rebuilt motor (about 8K on it) and has had good maintainence since the rebuild. I've replaced the fuel pump/injector, ignition mod and pickup coil...it will be getting new plugs and wires as well as an oil change...just wonder if there is anything else I should look for that isn't normally checked for basic day to day driving. thanks guys.  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||10-01-2000||03:21 PM||||Not engine/trans related, but check your spare to be sure it's got the required 60psi in it...<P>Air pressure in the temp spare is a lot more critical than in a normal spare.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||10-01-2000||04:26 PM||||A cell phone and aaa membership just in case.  I carry one of those 12V air pumps just in case.  I needed it once when a tire wouldn't hold air.  I got new rims and new tires, the old tires are still mounted on the rims and no air has leaked out in several years while just sitting. Go figure.<P>Have a nice drive from Ca to Tx.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ed||10-02-2000||12:53 AM||||I pack an extra engine belt, ignition module, and coil.  And fire extinguisher and tire inflator.  And screwdrivers and wrench and pliers.  <P>But I'm a worry wort.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||10-02-2000||09:06 AM||||I pack a set of tools to include combination wrenches, screwdrivers, channel lock pliers, monkey wrench, needle nose pliers, tire repair tools and plugs, fix'a'flat, WD-40, rope (for tieing a dead tire to the luggage rack), a gallon of anti-freeze, quart of motor oil, power steering fluid (just kidding), jumper cables, a tube of waterless hand cleaner,  flashlight, spare fuses, electrical tape,  a pair of cotton gloves (handy for reaching into a hot engine bay), an old work shirt, and other misc. items I can't think of right now.  If you turn your spare tire upside down (concave side of rim facing up) you'll gain a nice pocket to store stuff in.<P>I pack all the tools inside a soft side duffle bag like a sports bag.  I wrap all the tools in an old towel so they don't rattle and stuff the bag inside the spare tire rim opening.  It fits nicely and still gives room for storing the sunroof.<P>It might not hurt to keep your old ignition module handy also.<P>Good luck and have a fun safe trip,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000005||GT Bastard||10-02-2000||10:20 AM||||Oh yeah, and don't forget to get gas!  ||||reg||7
Z||000006||862M4inCA||10-03-2000||04:05 AM||||This IS a Fiero we're talking about....storage space is not one of it's most noted features.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Not to mention I have to pack EVERYTHING I can into it since it's a move on a very tight budget. I like the idea of turning the spare tire tho....definately something I didn't think of. As for the old ignition module someone mentioned....the old one left me dead on the road so I don't think that's going to help me much if this new one dies on me too.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I do appreciate all the ideas tho...some of which I haven't thought of since I keep telling myself that practically everything under the decklid is new/rebuilt. Was concerned about water hoses tho and wonder about those "fit all" emergency hose kits and wonder if they work or if I'd be wasting money and precious space by packing one of those also. BTW I think gas is a given. heh<BR>Thank for all the ideas again guys.<BR>                          Blake||||reg||1
Z||000007||capt_ron||10-03-2000||12:01 PM||||Here's what I've learned from driving coast to coast 3 times in a $500 pick-up truck. What ever you pack along as far as parts or tools it will be something giving you trouble that you didn't bring. I had one tire come apart and carb icing in the 15,000 miles that I travelled. Now I take only a few tools on my travels around the southeast in the Fiero. I believe that there is less strain, wear, and tear on the car buzzing along at a steady speed on the highway than around town stop and go driving. The best thing that should be along is experience and resourcefullness and a pocket full of cash or credit cards. You will have no problem on your trip, don't let the fear of breaking down tarnish the joy of sitting back and seeing the sights. After you get over the "jet lag" you'll think to yourself that it wasn't too bad after all. Have a nice trip.<P>------------------<BR>CAPT RON...<BR>Jack of Trades,<BR>Master of None.<BR>87 SE, 2.8L, Getrag.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Old Lar||10-03-2000||04:09 PM||||Duffle bags are the greatest to pack stuff into a Fiero.  They will conform to what ever shape is available.  If the seats can be moved forward slightly, there is lots of room to stack narrow stuff behind the seat.  I've only broke down twice, both times it was blown fuses.||||reg||1
